          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Do European and US bodies differ on when to start medication for stage 1 hypertension?",
          "answer": "Not medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.\n\nThe retrieved guidance shows a difference in how the two regions *label* blood pressure, but it does not give a side-by-side comparison of when each region starts medication for stage 1 hypertension.\n\n**Where the thresholds differ:** The guidance on diagnosis and blood-pressure targets (*Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets*) says the 130/80 mm Hg diagnostic threshold is a US position. European guidance starts office hypertension at 140/90 mm Hg and treats readings of 130 to 139 / 80 to 89 as high-normal rather than stage 1. The same reading can therefore be labeled differently depending on which body's guidance is used.\n\n**When drug treatment is considered for stage 1:** The guidance on when treatment starts (*Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line*) describes this approach:\n- If someone has established cardiovascular disease, diabetes, chronic kidney disease, or an estimated 10-year cardiovascular risk of 10 percent or higher, drug treatment is given alongside lifestyle measures.\n- If none of those apply, lifestyle measures come first, and the blood pressure is reassessed after three to six months before drug treatment is considered.\n\nThis passage does not say which region it reflects, so I can't confirm from it whether the US and European positions match on this point.\n\n**What is not covered:** The retrieved material does not contain a European treatment-initiation rule to compare with that one, so I can't say whether the two bodies differ on when to start drugs for stage 1.\n\nWhether any of this applies to you is a question for a clinician who can take your full history and readings. Taking the specific guideline you are interested in to that appointment would help.",
